  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 024: EUV dose, source power and stochastic defects connect pattern quality directly to scanner wafers-per-hour and effective lithography capacity.

Lesson 025: EUV availability converts installed scanners into productive capacity, so uptime and service can matter almost as much as tool count.

Lesson 026: High-NA EUV improves resolution by raising numerical aperture, but its value depends on whether it removes more manufacturing complexity than it introduces.

Lesson 027: EUV pellicles protect reusable reticles from repeating contamination defects, but their transmission and thermal behavior can affect scanner throughput.

Lesson 028: An EUV mask is a patterned multilayer mirror, so reflectivity, absorber geometry and buried defects all influence what is actually printed on the wafer.

The big idea

Yesterday we learned why an EUV mask can contain defects that are hard to see but still distort the printed image.

Today we add the manufacturing question:

**Which mask defects actually matter?**

A mask-inspection system can find suspicious features, but finding an imperfection is not the same as proving that it will print badly on a wafer.

The useful production loop is:

find a defect → judge whether it is printable → repair or reject it → verify the result

That distinction matters because a reticle is a reusable master pattern. A small defect that escapes qualification can repeat at the same location across many wafer exposures.

## 1. Detection and printability are different problems

Imagine a scratch on a projector slide.

A microscope might clearly see the scratch. But whether the audience sees it on the screen depends on the projector optics, illumination, focus and image contrast.

Mask inspection works similarly.

The inspection tool first asks:

**Is something on the mask different from what should be there?**

Then the fab must ask:

**Will that difference materially change the aerial image that the scanner projects onto photoresist?**

This second question is printability.

An inspection system that flags every harmless irregularity would create enormous review workload. A system that misses one genuinely printable defect can create repeating wafer yield loss.

So the goal is not maximum alarms. It is high capture of economically meaningful defects with manageable false positives.

## 2. Why EUV makes printability harder to judge

For a simple transmissive mask, it is tempting to think of defects as blocked or unblocked light.

EUV masks are more complicated because they are reflective multilayer optical systems.

A surface particle can block or scatter light. An absorber defect can change the intended pattern. A buried multilayer defect can alter the phase of the reflected EUV wave even when the top surface does not look dramatically damaged.

That means two defects with similar physical size can have very different wafer consequences depending on where they sit and how they interact with the scanner's illumination and optics.

This is why EUV mask control increasingly becomes an imaging problem, not merely a microscope problem.

## 3. Actinic inspection asks the mask the right optical question

**Actinic** inspection means using the same 13.5 nm wavelength used by EUV lithography.

That is valuable because some EUV-specific phase defects may behave differently under ordinary visible or deep-ultraviolet inspection than they do during actual EUV exposure.

Lasertec's ACTIS systems are an example of actinic patterned-mask inspection. They use EUV light to detect defects that can print on the wafer, including defects that are difficult to characterize with a different inspection wavelength.

